WEBVTT

00:00:02.299 --> 00:00:09.650
<v David Whitfield>Morning, everyone. Friday the 23rd of October. Can you hear me, or am I talking to the little spinning wheel again?</v>

00:00:10.539 --> 00:00:15.439
<v Fiona Doyle>We can hear you. Important opening observation: there's a birthday cake in the office.</v>

00:00:17.129 --> 00:00:23.079
<v Tomasz Nowak>That's my weekend plans sorted, then. Was going walking, but have you seen the weather? Rain sideways.</v>

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:28.469
<v Marcus Hale>So you're walking as far as the cake. Sensible risk assessment.</v>

00:00:29.899 --> 00:00:38.299
<v Tomasz Nowak>Warmer there than this portakabin. The heating in here is either tropical or absolutely nothing. Just moaning, not asking for a job on it.</v>

00:00:40.619 --> 00:00:46.570
<v David Whitfield>Right, before this becomes the cake and heating meeting. Finished bits first, then what actually needs doing.</v>

00:00:48.359 --> 00:00:54.659
<v David Whitfield>I already swapped the filter on the fire panel yesterday. That's finished, not another filter job for today.</v>

00:00:56.630 --> 00:01:02.579
<v Aisha Begum>Yeah, and I tested the IM3 yesterday and it's all fine now. Nothing outstanding from that test.</v>

00:01:03.219 --> 00:01:09.170
<v Sarah Okafor>I signed off the inspection on the fire alarm panel on Tuesday as well. That one's closed.</v>

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:14.450
<v Olumide Adeyemi>Lovely. Three things we don't need to go round again.</v>

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:21.489
<v Sarah Okafor>Less lovely, we're still waiting on parts for the line 3 chiller. Nothing we can do until they arrive.</v>

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:30.379
<v Sarah Okafor>And same story on the 11kV board, still waiting on parts. Nothing we can do until they arrive either.</v>

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.909
<v Fiona Doyle>Both still blocked, then. Not fresh actions for anyone this morning.</v>

00:01:38.099 --> 00:01:46.500
<v David Whitfield>Correct. Also, I noticed the palletiser was running a bit warm on nights, but it settled down. That's just an observation, not another job.</v>

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:52.000
<v Aisha Begum>Right, settled down. Keep that separate from anything we're actually agreeing.</v>

00:01:53.469 --> 00:01:59.420
<v David Whitfield>Marcus, can you check the guarding on the Palletiser this afternoon? That one's safety critical, straight away.</v>

00:01:59.969 --> 00:02:07.670
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, I'll check the guarding on the Palletiser this afternoon. Straight away when we get into that afternoon work, safety-critical one first.</v>

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.400
<v David Whitfield>Exactly. Not bundled in with that low-level warm-running observation.</v>

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:17.800
<v David Whitfield>Tomasz Nowak, can you swap the filter on the dryer today? It's urgent.</v>

00:02:18.259 --> 00:02:22.110
<v Tomasz Nowak>Yep, I'll swap the filter on the dryer today. Understood, urgent.</v>

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:27.569
<v Olumide Adeyemi>Sorry, you clipped there—different filter from the fire panel one you just mentioned?</v>

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:34.810
<v David Whitfield>Yeah, completely separate. Fire panel was yesterday and done. The dryer is the live job.</v>

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:42.810
<v David Whitfield>And I'll write up the near miss at the Boiler Safety Relief Valve this morning. That one's safety critical, straight away.</v>

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:49.120
<v Sarah Okafor>Thanks, Dave. The near-miss write-up is with you this morning, then.</v>

00:02:49.789 --> 00:02:52.939
<v David Whitfield>Yes, mine. Definitely not leaving that floating between us.</v>

00:02:55.110 --> 00:03:00.710
<v Marcus Hale>While we're on the team stuff, I'll organise the toolbox talk for the team next week.</v>

00:03:02.449 --> 00:03:05.949
<v Fiona Doyle>Cheers, Marc. Next week for organising the toolbox talk, yeah?</v>

00:03:07.139 --> 00:03:09.240
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, next week. I'll take that.</v>

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:20.479
<v Sarah Okafor>I'll sort the training records for the new starters by the 28th. That's a whenever you get a chance one, rather than drop everything, but still by the 28th.</v>

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:26.000
<v Fiona Doyle>Got you, Saz. Training records with you, by the 28th, around the higher-priority stuff.</v>

00:03:26.699 --> 00:03:29.500
<v Sarah Okafor>Exactly. It's mine, just not a stop-the-world job.</v>

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:42.979
<v Olumide Adeyemi>Um, the sensors on the line 1 mould temp need recalibrating first thing tomorrow. The priority wording on that is whenever you get a chance. I haven't got an owner for it.</v>

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.629
<v Aisha Begum>Sorry, first thing tomorrow, despite the whenever-you-get-a-chance bit?</v>

00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:56.830
<v Olumide Adeyemi>Yeah. Recalibrate the sensors on the line 1 mould temp, first thing tomorrow. That's the requested timing; it hasn't been marked urgent.</v>

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:03.009
<v David Whitfield>Right. Does anyone here actually have that, rather than us assuming somebody does?</v>

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:07.120
<v Tomasz Nowak>Not me. I can't take that one on.</v>

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:14.580
<v Olumide Adeyemi>And I'm raising it, not volunteering for it. Don't put my name against it just because I mentioned sensors.</v>

00:04:15.729 --> 00:04:20.629
<v David Whitfield>Fair point. Agreed it needs doing first thing tomorrow, but the recalibration is unassigned.</v>

00:04:22.579 --> 00:04:29.579
<v Fiona Doyle>Another gap: someone needs to schedule downtime for the palletiser with production after lunch. Low priority. That's not allocated either.</v>

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:38.040
<v Sarah Okafor>Low priority for the scheduling, yes. Not saying the guarding check is low priority—before those get mixed up.</v>

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:46.259
<v Fiona Doyle>Exactly. After lunch is when the scheduling needs doing, not me declaring a downtime slot after lunch.</v>

00:04:48.699 --> 00:04:51.149
<v David Whitfield>Understood. Anyone taking the production scheduling bit?</v>

00:04:52.649 --> 00:04:57.199
<v Sarah Okafor>I can't take it. Please don't assume shift management means it's automatically mine.</v>

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:01.220
<v Fiona Doyle>Nor am I accepting it here. I'm flagging the gap.</v>

00:05:03.699 --> 00:05:08.949
<v David Whitfield>Okay. Scheduling downtime for the palletiser with production, after lunch, low priority, still no owner.</v>

00:05:09.339 --> 00:05:17.040
<v David Whitfield>I will take the other conveyor one, though. I'll clear the jam and check the sensors on the outfeed conveyor by Thursday.</v>

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:23.420
<v Olumide Adeyemi>Davey, that's both clearing the jam and checking the sensors, by Thursday?</v>

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.339
<v David Whitfield>Yep, both with me, by Thursday.</v>

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:31.819
<v Aisha Begum>Right, outfeed conveyor. Separate entry from the palletiser discussion.</v>

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:36.600
<v David Whitfield>Yeah. Too many things ending in conveyor and panel this morning.</v>

00:05:37.629 --> 00:05:47.430
<v Marcus Hale>One more, and this isn't a casual one. We need someone to take vibration readings on the GT2 by 11am. Drop everything for that one. Nobody's taken it.</v>

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:52.920
<v Fiona Doyle>By 11am, highest urgency, but no name. That's a fairly big hole.</v>

00:05:53.819 --> 00:05:56.970
<v David Whitfield>It is. Can anyone actually accept the GT2 readings?</v>

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:03.500
<v Marcus Hale>I can't commit to it. I know I'm the one saying it, but I'm not taking ownership.</v>

00:06:04.759 --> 00:06:07.910
<v Olumide Adeyemi>I can't take it either. Sorry—talked over you there.</v>

00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:19.170
<v David Whitfield>No, you're all right. The GT2 vibration readings remain unowned. By 11am, drop everything for that one; the urgency hasn't gone away because there's no taker.</v>

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:28.819
<v Fiona Doyle>So the three gaps are the line 1 mould temp recalibration, the palletiser downtime scheduling, and the GT2 vibration readings. None accepted.</v>

00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:34.490
<v David Whitfield>That's right. Quick check on the things people have actually accepted, then we'll finish.</v>

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:40.120
<v Tomasz Nowak>The dryer filter is mine today, urgent. No confusion with yesterday's fire panel filter now.</v>

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:48.720
<v Marcus Hale>I've got the Palletiser guarding this afternoon, safety critical, straight away. And organising the toolbox talk for the team next week.</v>

00:06:49.899 --> 00:06:57.600
<v Sarah Okafor>Training records for the new starters with me by the 28th, whenever I get a chance. The two parts-blocked items stay blocked.</v>

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:08.649
<v David Whitfield>And mine are the Boiler Safety Relief Valve near-miss write-up this morning, safety critical, straight away; then the outfeed conveyor jam and sensor check by Thursday.</v>

00:07:10.139 --> 00:07:15.040
<v Aisha Begum>Nothing else from me. IM3 was the completed update, not something to carry forward.</v>

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:22.000
<v Fiona Doyle>Good. And the cake was information only. Although it may not survive this meeting.</v>

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:27.769
<v David Whitfield>Probably the clearest status we've had all morning. Thanks, everyone. We'll leave it there.</v>
